    <div class="row"><div class="col-lg-10 offset-lg-1">
      <h2 class="cs-font_36 text-center mb-5">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3 class="cs-font_20" style="margin-top:32px;font-weight:600;">What is cost per lead?</h3>
      <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#ddd;">Cost per lead (CPL) is the average amount you spend to generate one lead. It is calculated as total marketing spend divided by the number of leads generated in the same period. For example, USD 2,000 in spend that produces 40 leads gives a CPL of USD 50. Always read it alongside lead quality.</p>
      <h3 class="cs-font_20" style="margin-top:32px;font-weight:600;">What is the difference between CPL and CPA?</h3>
      <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#ddd;">CPL counts leads — people who showed interest, such as a form fill or a phone call. CPA (cost per acquisition) counts actual customers or completed conversions. A lead may never buy, so CPL is almost always lower than CPA. Tracking both shows whether cheap leads are actually turning into revenue.</p>
      <h3 class="cs-font_20" style="margin-top:32px;font-weight:600;">What is a good cost per lead?</h3>
      <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#ddd;">There is no universal number. CPL varies hugely by industry, market, channel, and offer, so external benchmarks are only a rough guide. Evaluate CPL against your own close rate and customer lifetime value instead: if leads close at 20 percent and a customer is worth USD 1,000, a USD 50 lead is excellent.</p>
